Draw a classic hand-drawn cartoon **explosion**, a 7-frame **sprite sheet** that
plays once, using only the drawing tool.

The explosion is a single blast that erupts and settles across seven frames: an
ignition spark, a bright white-hot flash core, an expanding orange fireball with
jagged flame tongues, a smoke-and-debris peak, then grey smoke thinning to nearly
empty by the last frame. It runs hot-to-cool — white and yellow hot early, orange
and ember through the middle, grey smoke late — and every frame is drawn on full
transparency so it composites over whatever is behind it.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. It
is the sole channel: you build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a
time**, each targeting a frame with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you
issue — recorded to a per-frame action log — are the **authoritative output that
is scored**, not the pixels on disk. An image produced any other way (writing a
PNG directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the
recorded operations count.

Each frame is its **own separate 48×48 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-circle --frame 1 --cx 24 --cy 24 --r 10 --color "#fffdf2"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the blast up operation by operation until each `frames/<index>.png` matches
the brief: a single centered explosion moving hot-to-cool from ignition spark (0)
to white-hot flash (1) to expanding fireball (2–3) to smoke-and-debris peak (4) to
dissipating smoke (5) and a nearly empty final frame (6), all on full transparency.
When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-frame action logs are your finished
submission.
